# Intro
A pulse of light crosses a periodic box of uniform dust-free gas (1e3 atom/cm^3,
0.1 Msun particles, 262144 particles, 51.8 smoothing lengths on a side) at a
set, slow light speed (3e-5 c, about 9 km/s). The pulse is a cube of 10
smoothing lengths, injected into the gas at the first step by the test
parameter `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_test_beam_1` (box corners, direction and
energy per particle in internal units; the layout of the 11 numbers is in
`examples/parameter_example.yml`). It is meant for tests, not for production runs.
`makeIC.py` writes the value to `beam_args.txt` and `run.sh` passes it to swift.
There is no star.

The light speed is set by `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed`:

- `cap`: a share of light arrives no earlier than the separation divided by the
  limit speed, `ISRF_packet_light_speed_fraction_of_c` times c, in the frame of the
  gas.
- `fixed`: as `cap`, and the time step of each gas particle is also bounded so that
  the light moves at the limit speed.
  `ISRF_packet_light_speed_time_step_margin` sets the bound.
- `unlimited`: a share arrives at the next step of its receiver, and the light can
  move faster than c (debugging only).

The example measures the speed of the pulse, and whether it depends on the
time-step hierarchy. `arm` selects the run:

- `one`: mode `cap` at 3e-5 c. All gas is on one time bin. 80 steps.
- `two`: mode `fixed` at 3e-5 c, margin 0.4305. About half of the gas takes half
  of the step, so there are two time bins. 96 steps, which is 48 base steps.

Both arms write a snapshot every 8 base steps (9.375e-6 internal units each).
`beamSpeed.py` takes the energy centroid of the light in flight along the beam
between the snapshots at 16 and 40 base steps, divides the advance by the time
difference and by the set speed, and gives the ratio of the two arms. A
smoothing-length hop per step makes the pulse slower than the set speed, so
the speeds are fractions of it; the ratio of the two arms is what tells whether
the time bins change the speed.

`params.yml` fixes the base time step at 9.375e-6 (internal units). Cooling is
off (`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`), so that the light is
the only thing that changes.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --enable-isrf-debugging-checks --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. `--enable-isrf-debugging-checks`
adds the `Packet*` snapshot fields that `beamSpeed.py` reads. The number of
reception slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). No download is provided. Pass
the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`; the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
arm=one ./run.sh
arm=two ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_<arm>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `arm`, `light_speed` (cap, fixed or unlimited),
`speed_fraction` (the fraction of c), `margin`, `n_steps`, `snapshot_every`
(base steps), `energy_scale`, `gas_density` (atom/cm^3), `gas_mass` (Msun),
`swift_exe`, `run_dir` and `extra_args` (further swift arguments).

# Measure
```
python3 beamSpeed.py run_one run_two
```
It prints the speed of each run over the set speed and the ratio of the second
run to the first. `--bar 0.05` makes it exit with status 1 if that ratio differs
from 1 by more than 0.05. `--fraction` gives the set speed if you changed
`speed_fraction`; `--first` and `--last` choose the snapshots of the window.
